\subsection{From Intro}


The foundation of a functioning democracy is its citizens' ability to participate in elections and ensure the orderly transfer of power. While Artificial Intelligence (AI) is often associated with concerns about misinformation and security during elections, chatbots offer a promising solution for improving voter participation. They provide essential information—voting dates, locations, and processes—in a user-friendly way that helps vulnerable populations, such as seniors \cite{senior-elections} and first-time voters, navigate the complexities of voting. However, concerns about generative AI's role in disseminating misinformation remain prevalent. For instance, OpenAI announced ChatGPT would defer election questions to human-curated FAQs during the 2024 elections despite its strong performance in QA settings \cite{openai-chatgpt-elections}. This emphasizes the need for specialized tools addressing election-related challenges specifically.

Researchers have assessed voting difficulty across U.S. states using metrics like the Cost of Voting Index (COVI) \cite{election-cost, COVI}, which ranks South Carolina among those with complex voting procedures due to its multi-tiered election processes covering municipal, state, and federal offices. The state's election commission oversees critical tasks such as voter registration and candidate certification. Given these complexities and the importance of empowering informed participation, tools simplifying access to reliable voting information are indispensable.

To address these challenges, we introduce \sca, a novel AI architecture designed to provide accurate, source-verified information through intent-based response generation and a provenance tracker. Unlike generative AI models prone to hallucination, \sca uses rule-based frameworks, sentiment-aware response mechanisms, and source tracking to ensure transparency and trustworthiness. As such, \sca powers \ebsc, a chatbot tool deployed to assist voters in South Carolina during the 2024 elections. This comprehensive tool was employed during South Carolina's 2024 elections at a university setting including students (first-time voters), faculty members, and staff—to explore chatbot effectiveness in addressing diverse queries related specifically to electoral matters. Our work builds on an earlier prototype deployed using 2022 data focused solely on seniors \cite{muppasani2023safe}. 

Through this work, we aim to highlight \sca’s contributions to improving domain-agnostic AI systems and demonstrate how \ebsc, powered by \sca, represents a scalable solution for addressing the evolving needs of elections and modern democracies. Beyond elections, \sca’s architecture offers a template for deploying AI systems in other sensitive domains, such as health, education, and public policy.



